    has a plain string contact and an extra allergies array). MongoDB accepts them because a collection
    has no fixed schema.
    insertOne() stores one document and insertMany() stores an array of documents. The database
    and collection are created on the first insert.
    find() with no condition displays all documents. pretty() prints them in a readable layout.

Question 4: MapReduce and Cassandra

MapReduce process
MapReduce processes large data in parallel in three phases. Example: calculating the number of
patients in each department of the patients collection.

  Phase                       Working in the example

  Map                         Each patient record is read and the map function emits (key, 1) where key =
                              department. Output: (Cardiology,1), (Orthopedics,1), (Cardiology,1),
                              (Pediatrics,1), (Neurology,1), (Neurology,1)

  Shuffle and sort            The pairs are grouped by key. Cardiology: [1,1], Orthopedics: [1], Pediatrics: [1],
                              Neurology: [1,1]

  Reduce                      The reduce function adds the values of each key. Final output: Cardiology = 2,
                              Orthopedics = 1, Pediatrics = 1, Neurology = 2

   MongoDB

   db.patients.mapReduce(
      function () { emit(this.department, 1); },
      function (key, values) { return Array.sum(values); },
      { out: "dept_count" }
   )
   db.dept_count.find()

Features of Apache Cassandra
    Distributed and decentralised. Data is spread over many nodes. There is no single master node,
    so there is no single point of failure.
    High availability. Data is replicated on several nodes, and the system keeps working even if a
    node fails.
    Linear scalability. More nodes can be added without downtime, and throughput grows with the
    nodes.
    Wide-column NoSQL model. Data is stored in tables with rows and column families, with a
    flexible schema.
    Fast writes and CQL. It handles heavy writes well and offers CQL, a query language similar to
    SQL.

Application
Cassandra suits a hospital network that must store huge, fast-growing patient records and monitoring
data across many servers with high availability.

Question 4: Star Schema vs Snowflake Schema

Introduction
A data warehouse stores data in a fact table (numeric measures such as amount) surrounded
by dimension tables (who, what, when). The way dimensions are arranged gives two
schemas. A star schema keeps each dimension as one denormalised table. A snowflake
schema normalises the dimensions into more tables (normalisation removes redundancy by
splitting a table into related tables).

Star Schema (diagram)

               Dim_Customer                                                                Dim_Product
          customer_id (PK)                                                            product_id (PK)
          name                                                                        product_name
          city                                                                        category
          state                                                                       brand
                                                        Fact_Sales
                                               sales_id (PK)
                                               customer_id (FK)
                                               product_id (FK)
                                               date_id (FK)
                                               amount

                                                        Dim_Date
                                               date_id (PK)
                                               order_date
                                               month
                                                year
Fact table in the centre, dimension tables joined directly. Dimensions are flat (denormalised).

Who bought: Dim_Customer. What was sold: Dim_Product. When: Dim_Date. City, state,
category, month and year sit inside the dimension tables.

Snowflake Schema (diagram)

                   Dim_City                                                           Dim_Category
          city_id (PK)                                                           category_id (PK)
          city_name                                                              category_name
          state_name
                                                       Fact_Sales
                                              sales_id (PK)
               Dim_Customer                                                           Dim_Product
                                              customer_id (FK)
          customer_id (PK)                                                       product_id (PK)
                                              product_id (FK)
          name                                                                   product_name
                                              date_id (FK)
          city_id (FK)                                                           category_id (FK)
                                              amount

                                                       Dim_Date
                                              date_id (PK)
                                              order_date
                                              month
                                            year
Dimensions are normalised further: Customer > City, Product > Category.

Differences (write any four)

  Point                   Star Schema                                     Snowflake Schema

  Structure               Fact table in the centre, dimensions            Dimensions split into sub-dimensions
                          joined directly (star shape).                   (snowflake shape).

  Dimension               Single, denormalised table per dimension.       Normalised into several related
  tables                                                                  tables.

  Redundancy              More redundancy (city, state repeat in          Less redundancy, data stored once.
                          every row).

  Complexity              Simple design, easy to understand.              Complex design, more tables and
                                                                          keys.

  Query                   Faster, fewer joins (2 in the example).         Slower, more joins (4 in the
  performance                                                             example).

Example with SQL (same data, same result)
Star schema: 3 dimension tables, query uses 2 joins.

  SQL

  DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS star_db;
  CREATE DATABASE star_db;
  USE star_db;

  CREATE TABLE Dim_Customer (customer_id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(50), city VARCHAR(30),
       state VARCHAR(30));
  CREATE TABLE Dim_Product (product_id INT PRIMARY KEY, product_name VARCHAR(50),
       category VARCHAR(30), brand VARCHAR(30));
  CREATE TABLE Dim_Date (date_id INT PRIMARY KEY, order_date DATE, month INT, year INT);
  CREATE TABLE Fact_Sales (
     sales_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
     customer_id INT, product_id INT, date_id INT, amount DECIMAL(10,2),
     FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES Dim_Customer(customer_id),
     FOREIGN KEY (product_id) REFERENCES Dim_Product(product_id),
     FOREIGN KEY (date_id) REFERENCES Dim_Date(date_id)
  );

  INSERT INTO Dim_Customer VALUES (1,'Rahul','Pune','Maharashtra'),(2,'Amit','Bhopal','MP');
  INSERT INTO Dim_Product VALUES (1,'Laptop','Electronics','Asus'),(2,'Pen','Stationery','Cello');
  INSERT INTO Dim_Date VALUES (1,'2026-11-01',11,2026),(2,'2026-12-01',12,2026);
  INSERT INTO Fact_Sales VALUES (1,1,1,1,50000),(2,2,2,2,100),(3,1,2,2,50);

  SELECT * FROM Dim_Customer;
  SELECT * FROM Dim_Product;
  SELECT * FROM Dim_Date;
  SELECT * FROM Fact_Sales;

  SELECT
      F.Sales_ID,
      C.Name,
      P.Category,
      C.State,
      F.Amount
  FROM Fact_Sales F
  JOIN Dim_Customer C ON F.Customer_ID = C.Customer_ID
  JOIN Dim_Product P ON F.Product_ID = P.Product_ID;

Snowflake schema
Snowflake schema: dimensions normalised, the same query needs 4 joins.

  SQL

  DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS snow_db;
  CREATE DATABASE snow_db;
  USE snow_db;

  CREATE TABLE Dim_City (city_id INT PRIMARY KEY, city_name VARCHAR(30), state_name VARCHAR(30));
  CREATE TABLE Dim_Customer (customer_id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(50), city_id INT,
     FOREIGN KEY (city_id) REFERENCES Dim_City(city_id));
  CREATE TABLE Dim_Category (category_id INT PRIMARY KEY, category_name VARCHAR(30));
  CREATE TABLE Dim_Product (product_id INT PRIMARY KEY, product_name VARCHAR(50), category_id INT,
     FOREIGN KEY (category_id) REFERENCES Dim_Category(category_id));
  CREATE TABLE Dim_Date (date_id INT PRIMARY KEY, order_date DATE, month INT, year INT);
  CREATE TABLE Fact_Sales (
     sales_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
     customer_id INT, product_id INT, date_id INT, amount DECIMAL(10,2),
     FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES Dim_Customer(customer_id),
     FOREIGN KEY (product_id) REFERENCES Dim_Product(product_id),
     FOREIGN KEY (date_id) REFERENCES Dim_Date(date_id)
  );

  INSERT INTO Dim_City VALUES (1,'Pune','Maharashtra'),(2,'Bhopal','MP');
  INSERT INTO Dim_Customer VALUES (1,'Rahul',1),(2,'Amit',2);
  INSERT INTO Dim_Category VALUES (1,'Electronics'),(2,'Stationery');
  INSERT INTO Dim_Product VALUES (1,'Laptop',1),(2,'Pen',2);
  INSERT INTO Dim_Date VALUES (1,'2026-11-01',11,2026),(2,'2026-12-01',12,2026);
  INSERT INTO Fact_Sales VALUES (1,1,1,1,50000),(2,2,2,2,100),(3,1,2,2,50);

  SELECT * FROM Dim_City;
  SELECT * FROM Dim_Customer;
  SELECT * FROM Dim_Category;
  SELECT * FROM Dim_Product;
  SELECT * FROM Dim_Date;
  SELECT * FROM Fact_Sales;

  SELECT
      F.Sales_ID,
      C.Name,
      Cat.Category_Name,
      Ci.State_Name,
      F.Amount
  FROM Fact_Sales F
  JOIN Dim_Customer C ON F.Customer_ID = C.Customer_ID
  JOIN Dim_City Ci ON C.City_ID = Ci.City_ID
  JOIN Dim_Product P ON F.Product_ID = P.Product_ID
  JOIN Dim_Category Cat ON P.Category_ID = Cat.Category_ID;

Conclusion
Star schema is simpler and faster for queries, snowflake saves storage and reduces
redundancy but needs more joins. The same question was answered with 2 joins in star and 4
joins in snowflake.

Question 6: SQL Queries and OLAP

Create the table and insert records

  SQL

  CREATE TABLE student(
     roll_no INT,
     student_name VARCHAR(30),
     department VARCHAR(20),
     city VARCHAR(20),
     marks INT
  );

  INSERT INTO student VALUES
  (1,'Swaroop','IT','Pune',98),
  (2,'Mayur','IT','Pune',78),
  (3,'Faras','CS','Nanded',48),
  (4,'Aditya','ENTC','Pune',68),
  (5,'Jayesh','ENTC','Bhopal',88);

(a) Display all records

  SQL

  SELECT * FROM student;

(b) Aggregate value

  SQL

  SELECT MAX(marks) AS max_marks FROM student;

(c) Group-wise value using GROUP BY

  SQL

  SELECT department, AVG(marks) AS avg_marks FROM student GROUP BY department;

Role of OLAP
OLAP (Online Analytical Processing) is used to analyse large amounts of data from different
points of view, on a data warehouse that stores historical data.

 • Multidimensional analysis. Student data can be viewed by dimensions such as
   department, city and marks, from multiple dimensions at once.
 • Roll-up and drill-down. Move between the institute-level summary and one department
   or one student.
 • Slice, dice and pivot. Slice gives one department, dice gives selected departments and
   cities, and pivot rotates the view.
 • Fast summaries. Maximum and average marks department-wise and city-wise are
   returned quickly.

 • Decision making. Compare departments and cities, and support quick, flexible analysis for
   decisions.

Question 7: Star Schema Example

Star schema of a e-commerce database
A star schema has one central fact table joined directly to flat dimension tables. The fact table holds
the measures and foreign keys. Each dimension holds descriptive data, so the diagram looks like a star.

                        Dim_Customer                                                    Dim_Product
                customer_id INT PK                                             product_id INT PK
                name VARCHAR                                                   product_name VARCHAR
                city VARCHAR                                                   category VARCHAR
                state VARCHAR                                                  brand VARCHAR

                                 1     who bought                                   what was sold 1
                                                    N                      N
                                                             Fact_Orders
                                              order_id INT PK
                                              customer_id INT FK
                                              product_id INT FK
                                              date_id INT FK
                                              quantity INT
                                              total_amount DECIMAL

                                                                      N
                                                                   when
                                                                  1
                                                          Dim_Date
                                              date_id INT PK
                                              order_date DATE
                                              month INT
                                              year INT

Fact table and dimension tables
   Fact table: Fact_Orders. It stores the measure (quantity and total_amount) and a foreign key to each
   dimension.
   Dimension 1: Dim_Customer. Describes the buyer: name, city and state.
   Dimension 2: Dim_Product. Describes the item ordered: name, category and brand.
   Dimension 3: Dim_Date. Describes the order date: day, month and year.

   SQL

   CREATE TABLE Dim_Customer (customer_id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(50), city VARCHAR(30), state
   VARCHAR(30));
   CREATE TABLE Dim_Product (product_id INT PRIMARY KEY, product_name VARCHAR(50), category VARCHAR(30),
   brand VARCHAR(30));
   CREATE TABLE Dim_Date (date_id INT PRIMARY KEY, order_date DATE, month INT, year INT);
   CREATE TABLE Fact_Orders (
      order_id INT PRIMARY KEY,
      customer_id INT, product_id INT, date_id INT,
      quantity INT, total_amount DECIMAL(10,2),
      FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES Dim_Customer(customer_id),
      FOREIGN KEY (product_id) REFERENCES Dim_Product(product_id),
      FOREIGN KEY (date_id) REFERENCES Dim_Date(date_id)
   );

Because the dimensions are flat, queries need fewer joins and run fast, which suits OLAP analysis.

Question 8: MongoDB Queries

   MongoDB

   use hospital
   db.patients.find()

   db.patients.find({department:"Cardiology"})

   db.patients.find({age:{$gt:40}})

   db.patients.find({age:{$gte:30,$lte:60}}).sort({age:1})

   db.patients.find({doctor_name:"Dr. Shah"},{_id:0,patient_name:1,age:1})

   db.patients.find({department:{$in:["Neurology","Pediatrics"]}}).sort({patient_name:1})

   db.patients.find({patient_name:/^A/})

   db.patients.find({"contact.city":"Pune"})

   db.patients.find({allergies:{$exists:true}})

   db.patients.find().sort({age:-1}).limit(3)

Operators and keywords used
    Retrieve: find() with and without conditions, projection and $exists.
    Filter: $gt, $gte, $lte, $in, a regex /^A/ and dot notation for contact.city.
    Sort: sort() with 1 for ascending and -1 for descending, and limit().
